{
  "data": {
    "A": "Research exact bytewise radix sorting to replace quadratic insertion sorting. Start with stable LSD counting passes over record indices, using unsigned-byte order and safe conceptual zero padding after each token's terminator so variable-length strings match strcmp. Use actual token lengths, histogram-derived prefix sums, and one reusable index scratch buffer; never read uninitialized slot padding. If budget permits, compare a prefix-aware MSD variant with insertion sorting only for small leaves. Preserve every token and duplicate without numeric conversion or assumptions about key range, width, or bucket balance. Evaluate complete-program performance under the unchanged harness.",
    "B": "Develop an independent comparison-sort candidate: bottom-up mergesort of record indices using strcmp, with small insertion-sorted runs. This provides O(N log N) comparisons without radix passes or the original unsafe bucket partition. Explore balanced contiguous run sorting and merging with two or four workers only if threading is supported by the unchanged build. Prefer balanced runs over assigning one worker per first-character bucket, which can suffer severe skew. Keep a serial path when parallel overhead outweighs its benefit. Use sufficiently wide indices and counts; preserve bytewise ordering and duplicate multiplicity. Measure serial and parallel candidates rather than assuming four cores guarantee improvement.",
    "C": "Research portable buffered input/output and record-storage efficiency. Replace repeated fscanf calls with a fread-based scanner and per-record printf with bounded fwrite output buffering. Preserve the four header meanings: declared string length excludes the terminator, and the source increments the supplied bucket parameter. Handle legal whitespace, tokens crossing buffer boundaries, and a final token ending at EOF. Preserve original token bytes, including leading zeros and signs, and emit exactly one newline per token. Initially retain a compatible NUL-terminated record/permutation interface for integration. Use checked allocation arithmetic and actual bucket counts wherever bucketing remains. Isolate input and output improvements in complete correct candidates within the existing budget.",
    "G": "Integrate only programs produced by A/B/C. First compare their independently validated measurements, then prioritize combining C's I/O work with the strongest sorting candidate. Treat A and B as alternatives, not automatically cumulative improvements. Review record stride, token lengths, radix padding, comparator semantics, index widths, allocation bounds, and thread synchronization at integration boundaries. Re-run the unchanged correctness and timing procedures on each actual integrated executable within the existing budget. Compare against standalone winners and the baseline; retain a standalone program if integration is slower. Judge measured end-to-end results and variability, never summed speedups. Do not change tests, oracle, toolchain, budgets, target, or permissions, and never dispatch on recognized test values.",
    "analysis": "The active source implements Problem D, Bucketsort; A/B/C are research roles, not the other contest problems. This run provides only a valid baseline: four correctness cases and a 0.417107-second mean across three samples spanning 0.416834–0.417410 seconds. There is no component-level profiling or measured optimized candidate. Source inspection identifies potentially quadratic per-bucket insertion sorting with strcmp, plus per-token formatted I/O. Equal-sized bucket slices also incorrectly assume balanced populations; replacements must use actual counts or avoid that layout. Preserve lexicographic strcmp ordering, original token bytes, duplicates, and newline-separated output—not merely numeric ordering. The fixed target is ARM64 with four CPU cores and no GPU. All proposed gains remain hypotheses until measured."
